            <div xml:id="d533">

                <pb facs="0482" n="400" sameAs="frus:frus1911/pg400"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/680.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Mexican Chargé d’Affaires.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1911-02-02"><hi rendition="#italic">February 2,
                        1911</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 407.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the embassy’s note of the 3d
                    ultimo, wherein this Department was informed that Abraham González, Cástulo
                    Herrera and P. J. Lomelí were engaged in seditious acts at El Paso, Tex., and
                    request was made that this Government take action against them for violating the
                    neutrality laws of the United States, I have now <pb facs="0483" n="401"
                        xml:id="pg401"/> the honor to inform you of the receipt of a letter dated
                    the 27th ultimo from the Acting Attorney General, stating that a copy of the
                    embassy’s note had been forwarded to the United States attorney for the western
                    district of Texas, with instructions to investigate the matter and institute
                    criminal proceedings against these men, if it is found that they have violated
                    the neutrality laws of the United States.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.],</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">P. C. Knox.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
